  "summary": "Marble courtyards, sunface marquetry and mirror halls — absolutism as décor.",
  "description": "The Louis XIV style is the visual language of absolutism: Versailles' marble courtyards, the Hall of Mirrors' arcade, Le Brun's painted apotheoses, Boulle marquetry (brass-and-tortoiseshell inlays), sun-face ornament (the king's emblem) and gardens where hedges obey geometry. Colbert's royal manufactories (Gobelins) industrialized luxury so French taste could be exported as policy; European courts copied the vocabulary for a century. The style's heaviness — massive commodes, gilt consoles, ceremonial ceilings — reads as power arithmetic: symmetry, axis, and the king everywhere implied.",
  "culturalContext": "Louis XIV's style made absolutism into décor: Versailles' grammar — axis, symmetry, sun-face — governed European court taste for a century, royal manufactories exported French luxury as policy, and the style remains the reference for \"palace\" in every visual medium.",
